\section{Notation}{

Argument names spell out what a quantity is rather than reproducing the
symbol used in any one textbook. Readers coming from the standard
references can map them as follows.

\tabular{lll}{
\strong{Argument} \tab \strong{Symbol} \tab \strong{Meaning} \cr
\code{icc} \tab \eqn{\delta} \tab Measure of homogeneity in the multistage
sampling variance decomposition. For two stages, \eqn{V_b/(V_b+V_w)},
in \eqn{[0, 1]} for nonnegative components with a positive sum.
Written \eqn{\delta} by Valliant, Dever, and Kreuter (2018).
Related to conventional ICCs and Kish's \emph{roh}, but not generally
numerically identical. See \code{\link[=varcomp]{varcomp()}} for the definitions. \cr
\code{var_ratio} \tab \eqn{k} \tab Ratio of the stage components' unit
variance to the analysis variable's. Defaults to 1. \cr
\code{unit_relvar} \tab \eqn{V} \tab Unit relvariance, \eqn{S^2/\bar{y}^2}{S^2/ybar^2},
that is the squared population coefficient of variation. \cr
\code{deff} \tab \eqn{DEFF} \tab Design effect. \cr
\code{n_psu} \tab \eqn{n_1} \tab Number of PSUs selected. \cr
\code{n_per_psu} \tab \eqn{n_2} \tab Units selected \emph{per} PSU, which is
a sample take, not the PSU's population size. \cr
\code{n_per_ssu} \tab \eqn{n_3} \tab Units selected per SSU. \cr
\code{moe} \tab \eqn{e} \tab Margin of error, the half-width of the
confidence interval. \cr
\code{cv} \tab \eqn{CV} \tab Coefficient of variation, the relative standard
error. \cr
\code{overlap}, \code{overlap_cor} \tab \eqn{\gamma}, \eqn{\rho} \tab Panel
overlap fraction and the correlation between occasions. \cr
\code{alloc_measure} \tab \eqn{X_h} \tab Positive stratum measure for Bankier
allocation, supplied as a frame column. Defaults to \eqn{N_h|\mu_h|}. \cr
\code{alloc_q} \tab \eqn{q} \tab Bankier allocation exponent, used only
when \code{alloc = "power"}. It is unrelated to statistical power. \cr
}

Dispersion may be given as either \code{var} or \code{sd} wherever a mean is being
planned, and exactly one is required. The exception is \code{\link[=n_twophase]{n_twophase()}},
whose frame
takes \code{sd} only.
